     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · BBC World · 2026-11-05 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>California Governor Newsom Commutes Death Sentences for All 548 Inmates</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#California</span> <span>#Gavin Newsom</span> <span>#Death Penalty</span> <span>#Politics</span></div>
       <p>California Governor Gavin Newsom has commuted the death sentences of all 548 individuals on death row in the state, converting them to life in prison. The decision, published on November 5, 2026, at 19:43 GMT, is largely symbolic in a state that has not executed a prisoner since 2006. California has historically maintained the largest death row population in the United States, yet only 13 inmates have been executed since voters reinstated the death penalty in 1978. Experts suggest this action could potentially save millions of dollars annually in court costs.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-05/california-governor-newsom-commutes-death-sentences-for-all-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Governor Newsom stated in a letter, &quot;We can now let these offenders die in obscurity while we forever remember and honour the victims.&quot; He is exercising his constitutional clemency authority to impose sentences of life without the possibility of parole. Newsom, who had previously ordered a halt to the death penalty in California in 2019, explained his decision, saying, &quot;After nearly 30 years in public office, I have come to understand the limits of what government can justly accomplish.&quot; He firmly believes that &quot;state-sanctioned killing is incompatible with California's values, undermines the foundations of our legal system and diminishes each of us.&quot;</p><p>Describing his decision as difficult but a &quot;righteous action,&quot; Newsom acknowledged that many Californians believe those who commit brutal crimes deserve to die, but he concluded that &quot;the state cannot morally kill them.&quot; He asserted that California's system for administering the death penalty &quot;is a failure,&quot; arguing it has not and cannot be fairly applied, leading to the conviction of innocent people. For Newsom, life without the possibility of parole represents the most severe punishment the state can lawfully impose and ethically enforce. He recognized that his clemency action would anger and hurt some Californians, and that it &quot;will always weigh heavily on me,&quot; accepting sole responsibility for the decision and its impact.</p><p>Newsom, a Democratic leader of the most populous U.S. state, is currently winding down his final months in office, being term-limited. His name has been discussed as a potential 2028 presidential contender, with fellow Democrat Xavier Becerra projected to succeed him after the upcoming midterm elections. While individual states hold the power to prosecute crimes and order executions, much of this authority rests with state governors. This contrasts with federal policy under former President Donald Trump, a staunch supporter of the death penalty, who described capital punishment as &quot;an essential tool for deterring and punishing those who would commit the most heinous crimes.&quot;</p><p>During his first term in 2020, Trump ended a nearly two-decade moratorium on the federal death penalty, ordering 13 executions over a six-month period. Although the moratorium was reinstated under President Biden, Trump signed an executive order reinstating the death penalty on the first day of his second term in 2025. No federal executions have been carried out since that order. In the U.S., both the federal government and local state authorities possess the power to order executions.</p><p>What to watch: Potential legal challenges or political ramifications stemming from Governor Newsom's executive action.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively integrates the source material and provides clear context. The mention of Xavier Becerra is sourced from the provided text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqrkyyd2j8mvo?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
