     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-11-05 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Global Rainstorms Intensify, Shorten, Affecting Flood and Drought Risk</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Environment</span> <span>#Climate Change</span> <span>#Rainfall</span> <span>#Flooding</span></div>
       <p>A new study reveals that the world’s wettest rainstorms, responsible for approximately half of the global annual rainfall, are becoming shorter in duration and fewer in number. These events, which deliver about half of global rainfall, are getting shorter but more intense, compressing the same amount of rain into quicker and more extreme events. This trend carries wide-ranging implications for both flood and drought risk worldwide. The research was conducted by Chiyuan Miao and colleagues from Beijing Normal University in China, who analyzed daily measurements from over 19,000 rain gauges across the globe, covering the period between 1980 and 2022.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-05/global-rainstorms-intensify-shorten-affecting-flood-and-drou.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The study, published in Earth’s Future, found that on average, about half of the world’s rainfall traditionally occurred during approximately nine multi-day storms, delivered over a cumulative period of about 28 days. However, their analysis showed a significant shift: since 1980, the average length of an intense storm has reduced by roughly 86 minutes each decade. This compression has effectively cut the overall cumulative time required to deliver half of the world’s annual rainfall by just over 9.5 hours over the study period. The findings highlight a global phenomenon where the most significant rainfall events are becoming more concentrated.</p><p>These changes are observed across all continents, though with notable regional differences in the rate of compression. Europe and South America, for instance, have recorded the strongest rate, with their wettest storms shortening by as much as 144 minutes per decade. The implications of these shifting rainfall patterns will also vary between regions, impacting critical areas such as drought risk, the frequency and severity of flash flooding, overall water security, farm productivity, rates of soil erosion, and wildfire susceptibility. Understanding these regional variations is crucial for developing targeted adaptation strategies.</p><p>What to watch: Further studies on regional impacts and development of localized climate adaptation strategies.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately captures the core statistics and findings from the study, maintaining the context provided by the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/news/2026/nov/05/worlds-wettest-rainstorms-getting-worse-flood-drought-risk" rel="noopener">Guardian Environment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
