     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · TechCrunch · 2026-11-05 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>OpenAI President Greg Brockman Signals Potential 2027 IPO</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#OpenAI</span> <span>#IPO</span> <span>#Greg Brockman</span> <span>#Enterprise Revenue</span></div>
       <p>OpenAI co-founder and president Greg Brockman indicated the company could be ready for an Initial Public Offering (IPO) in 2027. Speaking at a Wall Street Journal tech conference on Wednesday, Brockman affirmed his confidence in the business, citing accelerating enterprise revenue and a significant business turnaround. He stated, “We’ve been very clear this year that we want to go public when we feel is the right time for us, and I feel extremely good about the business.&quot;</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-05/openai-president-greg-brockman-signals-potential-2027-ipo.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Brockman detailed a changing trajectory for OpenAI's enterprise focus over 2026. He noted that the first and second quarters were &quot;pretty rough,&quot; and the company &quot;wasn’t even in the conversation&quot; regarding agentic coding, an area where rival Anthropic's Claude Code had gained market share. He acknowledged that while OpenAI had Codex, &quot;no one was talking about it.&quot; However, activity surged in the third quarter, with OpenAI experiencing 70% quarterly growth in its annualized revenue, a rise attributed to the enterprise side of the business doubling.</p><p>The majority of OpenAI's revenue now stems from its enterprise business, a significant shift from when it previously accounted for approximately 30% of total revenue. Brockman clarified that consumer revenue has also been growing, but the enterprise segment has &quot;really taken off,&quot; pointing to a recent partnership with J.P. Morgan Chase as an example of new client acquisitions. He added that the growth reflected a culmination of prior investments.</p><p>OpenAI has learned to help businesses identify key milestones with its AI technology, build trust, and demonstrate long-term reliability. Brockman highlighted the transformation, stating that a year ago, the company was primarily perceived as a consumer entity, facing questions about its enterprise commitment. He concluded, &quot;But it’s so different today.&quot;</p><p>What to watch: Continued growth in enterprise revenue and market perception ahead of a potential IPO.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately synthesizes the source material, capturing the key figures, the context of the IPO, and the specific business trajectory described by Brockman.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/05/openai-president-greg-brockman-says-company-could-be-ipo-ready-in-2027/" rel="noopener">TechCrunch</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
