     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-05 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Private Schools in Southwark Drive Social Class Segregation</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Research in the London Borough of Southwark reveals that private schools significantly reinforce social class segregation. Data shows 75% of students in the borough's three private secondary schools originate from the top two social classes, while public schools maintain a much higher concentration of students from lower-income backgrounds.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-05/private-schools-in-southwark-drive-social-class-segregation.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This distribution creates an &quot;index of dissimilarity&quot; where significant student movement would be required to achieve socioeconomic balance if private institutions are included. Experts argue this concentration exacerbates educational inequality, as social mixing is linked to improved attainment for lower-achieving students, whereas current systems hide class-based disparities in opportunity.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately reports the Southwark data and the broader implications for educational inequality mentioned in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-private-schools-segregate-students-class.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
