     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · Times of India · 2026-11-05 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Psychological Factors Explain Public Avoidance of Familiar Faces</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Individuals often avoid eye contact with acquaintances in public to prevent unplanned interactions, a behavior distinct from social anxiety. While some may be rushed, distracted, or simply unwilling to engage in spontaneous conversation, this action often aligns with the concept of &quot;civil inattention,&quot; where people acknowledge others without inviting further exchange.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-05/psychological-factors-explain-public-avoidance-of-familiar-f.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>However, research indicates anxiety can be a factor. A 2021 study involving 30 students found that those with higher levels of social anxiety exhibited increased avoidance of direct gaze when encountering others. Ultimately, the choice to look away serves as a social tool to control engagement levels during chance encounters.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately distinguishes between civil inattention and social anxiety, though it slightly conflates the 2021 study's focus on strangers with the broader context of avoiding acquaintances.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/life-style/people/psychology-says-people-who-pretend-not-to-see-someone-they-know-in-public-may-not-always-be-doing-so-because-of-social-anxiety/articleshow/135573009.cms" rel="noopener">Times of India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
