     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · India Today · 2026-11-05 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Rahul Gandhi Calls Strategy Meet Amidst Vote Chori Row; Summons Ministers, In-Charges Ahead of Diwali</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Politics</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Congress</span> <span>#Election Commission</span></div>
       <p>Congress leader Rahul Gandhi has convened a crucial strategy meeting at his Sunheri Bagh residence in New Delhi, scheduled for Friday evening on Dhanteras ahead of Diwali. The high-tea gathering aims to review ongoing political developments and map out the party's future strategy amidst the escalating showdown over alleged &quot;vote chori.&quot; State ministers, party general secretaries, and state in-charges from Congress-governed states like Karnataka, Telangana, and Himachal Pradesh, along with leaders from Kerala, are expected to attend. This meeting follows Gandhi's recent campaign visit to Sangrur in Punjab.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-05/rahul-gandhi-calls-strategy-meet-amidst-vote-chori-row-summo.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Concurrently, the Election Commission of India (ECI) faces mounting scrutiny, with an investigation flagging internal rifts and procedural concerns. Over the past five weeks, the poll panel has addressed eight key flashpoints, including delinking the Special Intensive Revision declaration from Form 6, mandating formal agendas and minutes, forming an independent IT audit panel, and granting field officers wider access. Opposition parties, however, have termed these actions as evidence of forced backtracking and an alleged cover-up, demanding Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar's resignation. The political battle has extended to the streets, as the BJP launches counter-offensives across states against the opposition's campaign.</p><p>In an unrelated development, the Board of Control for Cricket in India (BCCI) has shifted the first One Day International between India and Sri Lanka from Delhi to Visakhapatnam. The match, originally scheduled for December 13, was relocated following a request from the Delhi and District Cricket Association (DDCA), which cited potential weather disruptions, severe air pollution, and proposed renovation work at Arun Jaitley Stadium. Delhi has previously experienced disruptions during winter fixtures involving visiting teams like Sri Lanka and Bangladesh due to toxic air quality index levels.</p><p>Meanwhile, a significant political war of words has escalated in Tamil Nadu between the Tamilaga Vettri Kazhagam (TVK) and the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam (DMK). Former Chief Minister M.K. Stalin launched a direct attack on Chief Minister Vijay, questioning his governance, work ethic, and alleged reliance on astrologers, also citing law and order concerns and the seizure of contraband from a TVK functionary. In response, TVK national spokesperson Sathya Kumar defended the state government, claiming officers remained loyal to the DMK and accusing some officials of acting as opposition agents, while also highlighting TVK's anti-corruption drive. Political analyst Sushil Rajkumar labeled these explanations as excuses, contending the leadership failed to act decisively. The TVK, in turn, accused the DMK of double standards, referencing the controversy surrounding DMK leader Durai Murugan organizing his granddaughter's wedding according to Hindu rituals, despite the party's Dravidian ideology.</p><p>What to watch: Further political maneuvering ahead of key elections and ongoing administrative challenges.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: A comprehensive report that synthesizes all provided information, including the political developments, election commission issues, sports news, and the TVK-DMK conflict.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.indiatoday.in/india/video/rahul-gandhi-calls-strategy-meet-amidst-vote-chori-row-summons-ministers-in-charges-ahead-of-diwali-ytvd-3033190-2026-11-05?utm_source=rss" rel="noopener">India Today</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
