     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-05 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Study Finds Children's Fear of Sharks Increases With Age</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A University of Adelaide study published in Human Dimensions of Wildlife reveals that while 69% of children aged 2 to 12 like sharks, fear associations grow significantly as they age. Researchers surveyed parents of 374 children across 17 countries, finding that most children view sharks positively, often describing them as &quot;cool&quot; or good swimmers.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-05/study-finds-childrens-fear-of-sharks-increases-with-age.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Despite this early positive perception, 13.9% of children expressed fear, a sentiment unrelated to gender, location, or parental attitudes. Lead researcher Dr. Brianna Le Busque suggests that sensationalized media portrayals, which many children encounter via television and film, may influence these developing perceptions as they mature.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief provides an accurate summary of the study's findings and methodology while maintaining the correct nuance regarding media influence.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-young-children-sharks-age.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
