     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-05 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Webb Telescope's 'Little Red Dots' May Reveal Primordial Black Holes</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Astrophysics</span> <span>#James Webb Space Telescope</span> <span>#Black Holes</span> <span>#Early Universe</span></div>
       <p>Johns Hopkins researchers are focusing on &quot;little red dots&quot; observed by the James Webb Space Telescope as they investigate the potential existence of the universe's first black holes. This research, which tests a theoretical phenomenon, aims to shed light on how primordial black holes, for which astrophysicists have never found direct evidence, might have formed. The team's findings are published in the journal Physical Review Letters, offering insight into these objects that predate stars, galactic dust particles, and heavier elements.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-05/webb-telescopes-little-red-dots-may-reveal-primordial-black-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Using Webb Space Telescope data, the Johns Hopkins team specifically examined one such &quot;little red dot&quot; named QSO1 as a proof of concept. Scientists suspected QSO1 could contain a primordial black hole based on its age, size, and composition. It existed just 700 million years after the Big Bang and, unlike many other &quot;little red dots,&quot; is composed almost entirely of hydrogen and helium, the first elements formed during the universe's inception. This composition lacks the heavier elements that developed later in the universe's timeline. Co-author Valerio De Luca, a postdoctoral researcher in the Department of Physics and Astronomy at Johns Hopkins University, explained that while astrophysical black holes are born from the gravitational death of a star, primordial black holes are &quot;a very different story.&quot; He clarified, &quot;They come from the physics of the early universe that predates the first stars, when the universe was expanding exponentially in time and space. So the mechanisms for creating primordial black holes are different.&quot;</p><p>Since Stephen Hawking first proposed the idea of primordial black holes in the 1970s, scientists have sought evidence to prove their existence and distinguish them from astrophysical black holes. Astrophysicists have recently turned to swirling, superhot disks of galactic dust particles, energy, and gas—the &quot;little red dots&quot; on JWST images—to find answers. De Luca noted that while these dots &quot;could surround astrophysical black holes, and most probably many of them do, but they could also be primordial black holes, if primordial black holes exist.&quot; He added, &quot;So the question becomes, 'Which ones are primordial and how can we tell?'&quot;</p><p>A primordial black hole would only be able to grow to QSO1's size and obtain a swirling disk under special circumstances. Co-author Loris Del Grosso, also a postdoctoral researcher at Johns Hopkins, pointed out that &quot;There are constraints on primordial black holes from cosmological observations. There can only be so many primordial black holes in the universe, for example, and there cannot be too many of them in a given cluster.&quot; Researchers modeled three scenarios to determine if a primordial black hole could develop into a &quot;little red dot&quot; like QSO1, surrounded by its observed concentration of other disks. The most likely scenario indicated that a smaller primordial black hole pulled in and consumed surrounding gas and energy until it grew to QSO1's size. Del Grosso remarked, &quot;The door was open on this theory, that little red dots could be primordial black holes. We've now cracked it open a little further by showing some scenarios are more likely than others.&quot; The team plans to investigate how many of the 100 observed and cataloged &quot;little red dots&quot; might be primordial black holes. Definitive proof, according to the researchers, would require finding a black hole either too light to be an astrophysical black hole or so ancient it predates the existence of stars. De Luca concluded, &quot;If primordial black holes do exist, then something had to happen to make them in the first place, which would be a groundbreaking discovery in physics.&quot;</p><p>What to watch: Future investigations into the 100 cataloged little red dots for definitive evidence.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively covers the research findings, the methodology (QSO1 case study), and quotes the researchers accurately.</p>
